From: Adriel Santos <adriel.turox@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] remoteproc: qcom_q6v5_mpd: respawn the user PDs after a root PD crash

A firmware assert on IPQ5018 raises the root PD's fatal interrupt only.
The remoteproc core then recovers cd00000.remoteproc alone: rproc_stop()
with crashed=true, q6_wcss_stop(), rproc_start(). pd-1 and pd-2 are
separate rprocs, not subdevices of the root, so they keep reading
RPROC_RUNNING, the root keeps their two references, and nothing ever
asks the restarted firmware to spawn them again. The WLAN firmware QMI
servers live in the user PDs, so ath11k never gets FW_INIT_DONE after
the SERVER_EXIT the glink teardown gave it: CRASH_FLUSH stays set, every
WMI command fails with -ESHUTDOWN, and both radios stay dead until a
reboot (3 of 3 asserts on the bench, NSS and default images alike).

0821 does not help any more once the root has recovered: the root reads
RPROC_RUNNING again, so a later stop of a user PD sends the stop request
to firmware that never hosted it, times out after 5 s ("pd not stopped",
-110) and fails. That is what an ath11k hw-restart hit after an assert;
since ath11k_ahb_power_down() ignores the failure, each attempt also
leaks a user PD reference, which turned the watchdog's later sysfs
stop/start into a silent no-op (a separate ath11k bug, not fixed here).

Fix both halves.

Stale user PDs, always on: count root PD boots and record the count each
user PD was spawned under. A user PD spawned under an earlier boot is
stale, and wcss_pd_stop() skips the stop handshake for it the way 0821
skips it for a dead root, then does the SCM teardown and drops the
reference on the root. This alone should let a stop/start of each user
PD bring it back after a root crash, provided TZ accepts the internal
radio shutdown and MSA unlock against the restarted root.

Respawn, controlled by qcom_q6v5_mpd.respawn_userpds (bool, 0644,
default 1): when the root's stop runs for a crash, do the SCM half of a
user PD stop (internal radio shutdown for asid 1, MSA unlock) for every
user PD that is up and mark it torn down, before the root's own
pas_shutdown - the order of a normal stop. The respawn itself is an
rproc_subdev registered after the glink and ssr subdevs:
rproc_start_subdevices() walks the list in registration order, so its
start runs once glink is up again, and before the core marks the root
RPROC_RUNNING. It reloads each torn-down PD's segments, relocks its MSA,
spawns it and powers the internal radio up, which is the cold boot order
of wcss_pd_load() and wcss_pd_start(). A user PD that fails to respawn
stays torn down, and its eventual stop then only drops the reference on
the root instead of handshaking or unlocking again. With respawn off the
user PDs are left for their owner to restart through the stale path.

A respawn restarts the owner's in-place recovery, and firmware that
crashes the root again during that recovery would loop every few
seconds: crash, teardown, respawn, crash. The NSS Wi-Fi build's in-place
recovery raised a new root fatal (NOC) within 2 s in 3 of 3 bench runs,
and the remoteproc core has no recovery limit. So the respawn is
suppressed ("respawn suppressed after repeated root PD crashes") when
the root crashes within 60 s of the end of its last crash recovery, and
once qcom_q6v5_mpd.respawn_max_per_hour (uint, 0644, default 6)
respawns have been done in the current hour. Suppressed user PDs are
neither torn down nor respawned; they go through the stale path like
respawn_userpds=0, which ends such a loop with dead radios on a running
root, the behaviour before this patch.

upd_lock serialises all of this: wcss_pd_start() and wcss_pd_stop() hold
it across their whole SMP2P and SCM section, and the teardown decides on
the "up" flag set under it rather than on the unlocked rproc state, so a
user PD stop that is in flight when the root crashes cannot be unlocked
twice. It is never held while taking an rproc->lock; the reference on
the root is dropped after releasing it, since the root's recovery holds
its rproc->lock while it waits for upd_lock.

Whether the root firmware is alive is decided under upd_lock too, from
a root_up flag the driver owns: set with the boot count once
q6_wcss_start() sees the root ready, cleared when q6_wcss_stop() begins,
and combined with the root's q6.running (cleared by its fatal and
watchdog interrupts) and RPROC_CRASHED for the time before the stop.
The root's rproc state cannot be used for this, and 0821's read of it
on entry to wcss_pd_stop() is dropped: a recovering root reads
RPROC_OFFLINE until every subdev has started, so a stop that waited out
a respawn on upd_lock would otherwise skip the handshake and SCM-unlock
a user PD the firmware had just spawned, and a stop that read
RPROC_RUNNING before waiting would handshake with firmware that crashed
meanwhile.

q6_wcss_spawn_pd() now reinitialises spawn_done and start_done before
requesting a spawn. qcom_q6v5_prepare() is never called for a user PD,
so a completion banked by an earlier spawn, or an edge replayed after
qcom_smp2p_clear_last_value(), would let the waits succeed without the
firmware spawning anything.

A root PD that fails to restart is not recoverable without a reboot: the
core leaves it offline with the user PDs' references held, rproc_boot()
only bumps the count and rproc_shutdown() refuses a processor that is
not running. Say so where it happens (a failed start or stop during
recovery, a recovery whose subdevices failed to start), refuse to spawn
a user PD on a root that is not up instead of letting it time out, and
log the rproc_shutdown() error wcss_pd_stop() used to ignore.

Not run on hardware yet. CONFIG_QCOM_Q6V5_MPD is built in, so testing
needs a kernel image.

---
--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_mpd.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_mpd.c
@@ -12,6 +12,7 @@
 #include <linux/iopoll.h>
 #include <linux/kernel.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/mutex.h>
 #include <linux/of_address.h>
 #include <linux/of_device.h>
 #include <linux/of_platform.h>
@@ -44,6 +45,35 @@
 #define UPD_BOOT_INFO_HEADER_TYPE	0x2
 #define UPD_BOOT_INFO_SMEM_ID		507
 
+/*
+ * A root PD crash takes every user PD down with it, but the remoteproc core
+ * only recovers the root. When set, the user PDs that were up are torn down
+ * together with the root and spawned again once it is back. When clear, they
+ * are left for their owner (ath11k, sysfs) to stop and start; the stop then
+ * skips the handshake the restarted firmware cannot answer.
+ */
+static bool respawn_userpds = true;
+module_param(respawn_userpds, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(respawn_userpds,
+		 "Respawn the user PDs after the root PD recovers from a crash (default: 1)");
+
+/*
+ * A respawn restarts the owners' in-place recovery. Firmware that crashes the
+ * root PD again during that recovery would loop: crash, teardown, respawn,
+ * crash, every few seconds, and the remoteproc core has no limit of its own.
+ * So the respawn is suppressed when the root crashes within RESPAWN_HOLDOFF
+ * of its last crash recovery, and once respawn_max_per_hour respawns have
+ * been done within RESPAWN_PERIOD. The user PDs are then left to the stale
+ * path, as with respawn_userpds=0.
+ */
+#define RESPAWN_HOLDOFF		(60 * HZ)
+#define RESPAWN_PERIOD		(3600 * HZ)
+
+static unsigned int respawn_max_per_hour = 6;
+module_param(respawn_max_per_hour, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(respawn_max_per_hour,
+		 "Most user PD respawns within an hour before further ones are suppressed (default: 6)");
+
 enum q6_bootargs_version {
 	VERSION1 = 1,
 	VERSION2,
@@ -91,6 +121,39 @@
 	 * spawn bit and which therefore runs 1..MAX_UPD, not 0..MAX_UPD-1.
 	 */
 	struct userpd *upd[MAX_UPD + 1];
+	/*
+	 * Respawns the user PDs a root PD crash took down. Registered after
+	 * the glink and ssr subdevs, so the core starts it once glink is back
+	 * up and before it marks the root RPROC_RUNNING.
+	 */
+	struct rproc_subdev respawn_subdev;
+	/*
+	 * Serialises the user PD SCM and SMP2P sections of wcss_pd_start() and
+	 * wcss_pd_stop() with the root PD's crash teardown and respawn, and
+	 * protects the userpd fields marked (upd_lock). Never held while
+	 * taking an rproc->lock.
+	 */
+	struct mutex upd_lock;
+	/* bumped on every successful root PD start (upd_lock) */
+	unsigned int boot_gen;
+	/*
+	 * The root PD firmware is up: set together with boot_gen once
+	 * q6_wcss_start() sees it ready, cleared when q6_wcss_stop() begins
+	 * (upd_lock). The root's rproc->state cannot stand in for it: the core
+	 * leaves a recovering root RPROC_OFFLINE until every subdev has
+	 * started, which includes the respawn of live user PDs.
+	 */
+	bool root_up;
+	/* a root PD crash recovery is in progress (root rproc->lock) */
+	bool recovering;
+	/* this recovery tore user PDs down for a respawn (root rproc->lock) */
+	bool respawn_armed;
+	/* the last crash recovery ended at recovered_at (root rproc->lock) */
+	bool recovered_once;
+	unsigned long recovered_at;
+	/* respawns done in the period starting at respawn_period_start (same) */
+	unsigned long respawn_period_start;
+	unsigned int respawn_period_cnt;
 };
 
 struct userpd {
@@ -98,6 +161,16 @@
 	struct device *dev;
 	struct qcom_rproc_ssr ssr_subdev;
 	struct qcom_q6v5 q6;
+	/* root PD boot_gen this user PD was last spawned under (upd_lock) */
+	unsigned int spawned_gen;
+	/* spawned with its MSA locked, SCM teardown still owed (upd_lock) */
+	bool up;
+	/*
+	 * A root PD crash tore this user PD down underneath the remoteproc
+	 * core, which still sees it running: waiting for the respawn, or for
+	 * the core to stop it (upd_lock).
+	 */
+	bool torn_down;
 };
 
 struct wcss_data {
@@ -120,6 +193,38 @@
 	return bit / 8;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The root PD did not come back from a crash. The remoteproc core leaves it
+ * offline (or still crashed) with the user PDs' references on it held, so
+ * nothing short of a reboot restarts it: rproc_boot() only bumps the refcount
+ * and rproc_shutdown() refuses a processor that is not running. Any user PD
+ * torn down with it stays torn down.
+ */
+static void q6_wcss_recovery_failed(struct q6_wcss *wcss)
+{
+	wcss->recovering = false;
+	wcss->respawn_armed = false;
+	dev_err(wcss->dev,
+		"root PD did not recover from the crash, the user PDs stay down until reboot\n");
+}
+
+/*
+ * Whether the root PD firmware is gone as far as a user PD is concerned, i.e.
+ * a stop request cannot be answered and a spawn request cannot succeed.
+ * Called with upd_lock held, so root_up cannot change underneath: it is false
+ * from the start of a root stop until the next successful root start, even
+ * while the core still reads the recovering root as RPROC_OFFLINE. Before the
+ * root's stop runs, the root's fatal and watchdog interrupts clear q6.running,
+ * and the crash handler sets RPROC_CRASHED.
+ */
+static bool q6_wcss_root_dead(struct q6_wcss *wcss)
+{
+	lockdep_assert_held(&wcss->upd_lock);
+
+	return !wcss->root_up || !READ_ONCE(wcss->q6.running) ||
+	       READ_ONCE(wcss->q6.rproc->state) == RPROC_CRASHED;
+}
+
 static int q6_wcss_start(struct rproc *rproc)
 {
 	struct q6_wcss *wcss = rproc->priv;
@@ -146,10 +251,17 @@
 		goto unprepare;
 	}
 
+	mutex_lock(&wcss->upd_lock);
+	wcss->boot_gen++;
+	wcss->root_up = true;
+	mutex_unlock(&wcss->upd_lock);
+
 	return 0;
 
 unprepare:
 	qcom_q6v5_unprepare(&wcss->q6);
+	if (wcss->recovering)
+		q6_wcss_recovery_failed(wcss);
 	return ret;
 }
 
@@ -158,6 +270,15 @@
 	int ret;
 	struct userpd *upd = rproc->priv;
 
+	/*
+	 * qcom_q6v5_prepare() is never called for a user PD, so nothing else
+	 * rearms these: a completion left over from an earlier spawn, or an
+	 * edge replayed after qcom_smp2p_clear_last_value(), would let the
+	 * waits below succeed without the firmware having spawned anything.
+	 */
+	reinit_completion(&upd->q6.spawn_done);
+	reinit_completion(&upd->q6.start_done);
+
 	ret = qcom_q6v5_request_spawn(&upd->q6);
 	if (ret == -ETIMEDOUT) {
 		dev_err(upd->dev, "%s spawn timedout\n", rproc->name);
@@ -182,49 +303,283 @@
 	u32 pasid = (upd->pd_asid << 8) | UPD_SWID;
 	int ret;
 
+	mutex_lock(&wcss->upd_lock);
+
+	/*
+	 * The root crashed after wcss_pd_load() took its reference. A spawn
+	 * could only time out, holding upd_lock for up to 15 s while the root's
+	 * recovery waits for it.
+	 */
+	if (q6_wcss_root_dead(wcss)) {
+		dev_err(upd->dev, "%s: root PD is down, not spawning\n",
+			rproc->name);
+		ret = -ENODEV;
+		goto unlock;
+	}
+
 	ret = qcom_scm_msa_lock(pasid);
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(upd->dev, "failed to power up pd\n");
-		return ret;
+		goto unlock;
 	}
 
 	if (upd->q6.spawn_bit) {
 		ret = q6_wcss_spawn_pd(rproc);
 		if (ret)
-			return ret;
+			goto unlock;
 	}
 
 	if (upd->pd_asid == 1) {
 		ret = qcom_scm_internal_wifi_powerup(wcss->desc->pasid);
 		if (ret) {
 			dev_err(upd->dev, "failed to power up internal radio\n");
-			return ret;
+			goto unlock;
 		}
 	}
 
+	upd->spawned_gen = wcss->boot_gen;
+	upd->torn_down = false;
+	upd->up = true;
+
+unlock:
+	mutex_unlock(&wcss->upd_lock);
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/* Called from the root PD's crash stop, with upd_lock held. */
+static bool q6_wcss_respawn_allowed(struct q6_wcss *wcss)
+{
+	unsigned long now = jiffies;
+	unsigned int max = READ_ONCE(respawn_max_per_hour);
+
+	if (!READ_ONCE(respawn_userpds)) {
+		dev_info(wcss->dev,
+			 "user PD respawn disabled, user PDs stay down until restarted\n");
+		return false;
+	}
+
+	if (wcss->recovered_once &&
+	    time_before(now, wcss->recovered_at + RESPAWN_HOLDOFF)) {
+		dev_err(wcss->dev,
+			"root PD crashed %u ms after its last recovery: respawn suppressed after repeated root PD crashes, user PDs stay down until restarted\n",
+			jiffies_to_msecs(now - wcss->recovered_at));
+		return false;
+	}
+
+	if (!wcss->respawn_period_cnt ||
+	    time_after_eq(now, wcss->respawn_period_start + RESPAWN_PERIOD)) {
+		wcss->respawn_period_start = now;
+		wcss->respawn_period_cnt = 0;
+	}
+	if (wcss->respawn_period_cnt >= max) {
+		dev_err(wcss->dev,
+			"%u user PD respawns within the hour: respawn suppressed after repeated root PD crashes, user PDs stay down until restarted\n",
+			wcss->respawn_period_cnt);
+		return false;
+	}
+	wcss->respawn_period_cnt++;
+
+	return true;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Called from the root PD's stop while the remoteproc core recovers it from a
+ * crash. The user PDs died with the root, but the core only knows about the
+ * root: do the SCM half of wcss_pd_stop() for each one that is up, without
+ * the stop handshake nobody can answer, and mark it for respawn. Its rproc
+ * state and its reference on the root are left alone, which keeps the core's
+ * view consistent with the respawn q6_wcss_respawn_start() performs.
+ *
+ * This runs before the root's own pas_shutdown, the same order as a normal
+ * stop of every user PD followed by the root.
+ *
+ * With respawn disabled or suppressed nothing is torn down, and the user PDs
+ * are left for the stale path. Returns whether a respawn is owed.
+ */
+static bool q6_wcss_teardown_userpds(struct q6_wcss *wcss)
+{
+	struct userpd *upd;
+	bool owed = false;
+	u32 pasid;
+	int i, ret;
+
+	mutex_lock(&wcss->upd_lock);
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(wcss->upd); i++) {
+		upd = wcss->upd[i];
+		if (!upd || (!upd->up && !upd->torn_down))
+			continue;
+		/* the dying firmware's fatal edges must not start a PD recovery */
+		upd->q6.running = false;
+		owed = true;
+	}
+
+	if (!owed || !q6_wcss_respawn_allowed(wcss)) {
+		mutex_unlock(&wcss->upd_lock);
+		return false;
+	}
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(wcss->upd); i++) {
+		upd = wcss->upd[i];
+		if (!upd || !upd->up)
+			continue;
+
+		pasid = (upd->pd_asid << 8) | UPD_SWID;
+
+		if (upd->pd_asid == 1) {
+			ret = qcom_scm_internal_wifi_shutdown(wcss->desc->pasid);
+			if (ret)
+				dev_warn(upd->dev,
+					 "failed to power down internal radio: %d\n",
+					 ret);
+		}
+
+		ret = qcom_scm_msa_unlock(pasid);
+		if (ret)
+			dev_warn(upd->dev, "failed to power down pd: %d\n", ret);
+
+		upd->up = false;
+		upd->torn_down = true;
+		dev_info(upd->dev, "%s: torn down with crashed root PD\n",
+			 upd->q6.rproc->name);
+	}
+	mutex_unlock(&wcss->upd_lock);
+
+	return true;
+}
+
+/* The cold boot order: wcss_pd_load(), then the body of wcss_pd_start(). */
+static void q6_wcss_respawn_userpds(struct q6_wcss *wcss)
+{
+	const struct firmware *fw;
+	struct userpd *upd;
+	struct rproc *urproc;
+	u32 pasid;
+	int i, ret;
+
+	mutex_lock(&wcss->upd_lock);
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(wcss->upd); i++) {
+		upd = wcss->upd[i];
+		if (!upd || !upd->torn_down)
+			continue;
+
+		urproc = upd->q6.rproc;
+		pasid = (upd->pd_asid << 8) | UPD_SWID;
+
+		/*
+		 * The root died again during its own start. The core ignores
+		 * that crash (it still reads RPROC_OFFLINE), and a spawn could
+		 * only time out.
+		 */
+		if (q6_wcss_root_dead(wcss)) {
+			dev_err(upd->dev,
+				"%s: root PD down again, respawn abandoned\n",
+				urproc->name);
+			continue;
+		}
+
+		ret = request_firmware(&fw, urproc->firmware, upd->dev);
+		if (!ret) {
+			ret = qcom_mdt_load_pd_seg(upd->dev, fw, urproc->firmware,
+						   wcss->desc->pasid, upd->pd_asid,
+						   wcss->mem_region, wcss->mem_phys,
+						   wcss->mem_size, NULL);
+			release_firmware(fw);
+		}
+		if (ret) {
+			dev_err(upd->dev,
+				"%s: reload after root PD recovery failed: %d\n",
+				urproc->name, ret);
+			continue;
+		}
+
+		ret = qcom_scm_msa_lock(pasid);
+		if (ret) {
+			dev_err(upd->dev,
+				"%s: msa lock after root PD recovery failed: %d\n",
+				urproc->name, ret);
+			continue;
+		}
+
+		if (upd->q6.spawn_bit)
+			ret = q6_wcss_spawn_pd(urproc);
+		if (!ret && upd->pd_asid == 1)
+			ret = qcom_scm_internal_wifi_powerup(wcss->desc->pasid);
+		if (ret) {
+			dev_err(upd->dev,
+				"%s: respawn after root PD recovery failed: %d\n",
+				urproc->name, ret);
+			upd->q6.running = false;
+			qcom_scm_msa_unlock(pasid);
+			/* stays torn_down: its stop must not handshake or unlock */
+			continue;
+		}
+
+		upd->spawned_gen = wcss->boot_gen;
+		upd->torn_down = false;
+		upd->up = true;
+		dev_info(upd->dev, "%s: respawned after root PD recovery\n",
+			 urproc->name);
+	}
+	mutex_unlock(&wcss->upd_lock);
+}
+
+static int q6_wcss_respawn_start(struct rproc_subdev *subdev)
+{
+	struct q6_wcss *wcss = container_of(subdev, struct q6_wcss,
+					    respawn_subdev);
+
+	if (wcss->recovering) {
+		wcss->recovering = false;
+		if (wcss->respawn_armed)
+			q6_wcss_respawn_userpds(wcss);
+		wcss->respawn_armed = false;
+		/* the holdoff runs from here, suppressed respawn or not */
+		wcss->recovered_at = jiffies;
+		wcss->recovered_once = true;
+	}
+
+	/* the root itself is up: a user PD that failed to respawn stays down */
+	return 0;
+}
+
 static int q6_wcss_stop(struct rproc *rproc)
 {
 	struct q6_wcss *wcss = rproc->priv;
 	const struct wcss_data *desc = wcss->desc;
+	bool crashed = rproc->state == RPROC_CRASHED;
 	int ret;
 
+	mutex_lock(&wcss->upd_lock);
+	wcss->root_up = false;
+	mutex_unlock(&wcss->upd_lock);
+
+	if (crashed) {
+		wcss->recovering = true;
+		wcss->respawn_armed = q6_wcss_teardown_userpds(wcss);
+	} else if (wcss->recovering) {
+		/* rproc_start() unwinding a recovery whose subdevices failed */
+		q6_wcss_recovery_failed(wcss);
+	}
+
 	ret = qcom_q6v5_request_stop(&wcss->q6, NULL);
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(wcss->dev, "pd not stopped\n");
-		return ret;
+		goto err;
 	}
 
 	ret = qcom_scm_pas_shutdown(desc->pasid);
 	if (ret) {
 		dev_err(wcss->dev, "not able to shutdown\n");
-		return ret;
+		goto err;
 	}
 	qcom_q6v5_unprepare(&wcss->q6);
 
 	return 0;
+
+err:
+	if (crashed)
+		q6_wcss_recovery_failed(wcss);
+	return ret;
 }
 
 /**
@@ -258,11 +613,43 @@
 	struct rproc *rpd_rproc = dev_get_drvdata(upd->dev->parent);
 	struct q6_wcss *wcss = rpd_rproc->priv;
 	u32 pasid = (upd->pd_asid << 8) | UPD_SWID;
-	bool rootpd_dead = rpd_rproc->state == RPROC_CRASHED ||
-			   rpd_rproc->state == RPROC_OFFLINE;
+	bool rootpd_dead, stale;
 	int ret;
 
 	/*
+	 * Everything below is decided under upd_lock. A stop can wait here
+	 * for a whole root crash teardown and respawn, or behind another
+	 * PD's stop handshake while the root crashes, so the root's state
+	 * seen on entry says nothing about the firmware found once the lock
+	 * is held.
+	 */
+	mutex_lock(&wcss->upd_lock);
+
+	/*
+	 * q6_wcss_teardown_userpds() already did the SCM half of this stop
+	 * when the root PD crashed, and no respawn has happened since (or it
+	 * failed), so the running root does not host this PD. Only the
+	 * reference on the root is left to drop.
+	 */
